      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1925 the Grand Ole Opry opened, launching Nashville’s reputation as the Country Music Capital of the World.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nashville%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Fisk Jubilee Singers emerged after the Civil War, turning Fisk University into a beacon of hope and cultural pride.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nashville%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Nashville’s Country Music Hall of Fame and Museum, Bridgestone Arena, and Nissan Stadium were all renovated or built during Governor Bredesen’s urban renewal push.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
